
# Setup a python virtual environment

Note: you want setup a service pls refer to the readme further below. To start and try, these are the right steps

```
python -m venv ./venv
source ./venv/bin/activate
```
# Install the requirements

```
pip install -r requirements.txt

```

# Configure UdpPipe



## Head

From the scripts directory copy the template for head. Unless you have some special setting,
this can be used w/o any modifications.


Launch Command is something like:

```
python3 eu/liebrand/UdpPipe/Pipe.py --head
```

+ open the firewall port for tcp traffic (for the port specified in the ini default 16001)

## Tail

Tail usually requires more adjustments as the services are to be specified.

While tail connected to head via TCP using the specified port, the service supported use UDP

+ Configure the remote host and port (host is the host tail is running on, port is the port on the
head host.

For each service you have to specify the following:
+ listening port on the host where head runs (you must open the firewall, if you want to see packets
from other than localhost)
+ Host in your LAN offering the service (e.g. runs the VPN server)
+ Port on that host where the UDP traffic should be sent to.

```
python3 eu/liebrand/UdpPipe/Pipe.py --tail
```


# Testing

+ configure the sample "TEST" service
+ on tail: start udptest -s 12345
+ on head: start udptest <host> <port> (where post is the listenport configured in head's udppipe.ini)

# Setting up as a Service

Create a separate user

```
sudo useradd -m udppipe
```

su to the user and
+ clone the repo
+ create a venv
+ install the requirement packages

+ Copy the launch script to UdpPipe directory (head or tail)
+ Adjust the path(s)
+ disable logging to console

Adjust the udppipe.service to your needs:
+ user udppipe
+ path to the start script

copy the udppipe.service to /etc/systemd/system

start the service:

```
systemctl start udppipe.service
```

to make the service starting permanent:

```
systemctl enable udpppipe.service
```

